<details class="faq-item"><summary>How long does cycling really take?</summary><p>Typically 4–8 weeks unassisted, and 2–6 weeks with bottled bacteria. It can be as little as one to two weeks if you seed the filter with media from an established tank. The only thing that tells you it''s finished is your test kit: 2 ppm of ammonia processed to 0 ammonia and 0 nitrite within 24 hours, with nitrate present.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Can I use bottled bacteria and add fish the same day?</summary><p>Some products claim this and we''d be cautious. Bottled bacteria genuinely help and can shorten cycling substantially, but "instant" results depend on the bottle being fresh, correctly stored and correctly dosed — and you have no way to verify that. If you do try it, add only one or two hardy fish, test daily, and be ready to do water changes.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Where do the bacteria actually live?</summary><p>Mostly in your filter media — that''s why filter maintenance is the most dangerous routine job in fishkeeping. They also colonise substrate, decor and glass, but the filter holds the overwhelming majority, because it has constant oxygenated flow. Never replace all your media at once; if a cartridge must be changed, run the old and new together for a few weeks first.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Do live plants replace the need for water changes?</summary><p>No. Plants consume nitrate and help a great deal — a heavily planted tank is more stable and grows less algae — but they don''t remove everything that accumulates, and they can''t replace the mineral replenishment a water change provides. Keep changing water weekly.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>What''s the best first fish for a beginner?</summary><p>In a cycled 20 gallon: harlequin rasbora, platies, cherry barbs, zebra danios or a corydoras group are all hardy, peaceful and forgiving. Avoid starting with anything delicate — neons and cardinals are lovely but genuinely sensitive to imperfect water, so save them for a tank that''s been running a few months.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>My tank has been fine for months and ammonia suddenly appeared. Why?</summary><p>Almost always one of five things: you replaced or rinsed filter media in tap water, you added too many fish at once, you medicated the tank, the filter stopped for a period, or something has died and is decaying out of sight. Check behind decor for a missing fish, and treat it as a fish-in cycle until readings recover.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Can I use tap water?</summary><p>Yes, with a dechlorinator — that''s what they''re for. Just never add untreated tap water to a tank with fish or an established filter: chlorine and chloramine kill fish and your bacteria colony. If you''re on well water, test it before use; nitrate levels in some well supplies are high enough to matter.</p></details>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Fishkeeping isn''t difficult, but it is unforgiving of the one shortcut everyone is encouraged to take. <strong>Cycle the tank before you buy a single fish.</strong> Buy a liquid test kit, dose ammonia, add bacteria, and wait the four to eight weeks — it''s the least glamorous month in the hobby and the one that determines whether everything afterwards works.</p>
<p>Then go bigger than you were planning. A 20 long is easier than a 10, a 40 breeder is easier than a 20, and a 75 practically runs itself by comparison. More water means more stability, more room for fish that behave naturally, and far more forgiveness when — not if — you make a mistake.</p>
